          %title Initiality Project - Type Theory
          %h1#initiality_project__type_theory Initiality Project - Type Theory
          %p
            This page is part of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Initiality+Project"} Initiality Project
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#bidirectionality"} Bidirectionality
              %li
                %a{:href => "#contexts"} Contexts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#judgements"} Judgment forms
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#modes_preconditions_etc"} Modes, preconditions, etc.
              %li
                %a{:href => "#primitive_rules"} Primitive rules
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#structural_rules"} Structural rules
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#modeswitching_rule"} Mode-switching rule
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#equality_rules"} Equality rules
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#constant_rules"} Constant rules
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#type_former_rules"} Type former rules
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#types"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Π
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                    \-types
              %li
                %a{:href => "#valid_contexts_and_telescopes"} Valid Contexts and Telescopes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#bidirectional_vs_unidirectional"} Bidirectional vs Unidirectional
              %li
                %a{:href => "#admissible_rules"} Admissible rules
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#inversion_lemmas"} Inversion lemmas
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#structural_rules_2"} Structural rules
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#sanity_check"} Sanity check
          %h2#bidirectionality Bidirectionality
          %p
            All our terms are fully annotated. For instance, we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi App
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
            rather than just
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi App
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App(M,N)
          %p
            In particular, this means that given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T:A
            can be derived for any value of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            that can be deduced syntactically, or
            = succeed "," do
              %em synthesized
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi App
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
            has any type, then it must have the type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B[N/x]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash T:A \qquad \Gamma \vdash A\equiv B \,type}{\Gamma \vdash T:B}
          %p
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi App
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
            must also have any type that is judgmentally
            %em equal
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B[N/x]
            %em has
            these types, there is a sense in which it “has” them in a different sense from the one unique one that it synthesizes. Moreover, the conversion rule is perhaps the ickiest thing to deal with in an initiality theorem, because it can be applied absolutely anywhere, over and over again, leading to many many different derivations of the same judgment that should not be distinguished semantically.
          %p
            Note that since all our terms are fully annotated, all of our formation, introduction, and elimination rules can synthesize their types. However, the type annotations also tell us what types the subterms need to have (i.e. the types that must occur in the premises of the corresponding rule), in order for the term to have its canonical type. These types may not be
            %em syntactically
            equal to the types synthesized by the subterms, so we need to
            %em check
            subterms against the types we want. The check should succeed if and only if the subterm’s synthesized type is
            %em judgmentally
            equal to the one we want; and this is the only place that the conversion rule really
            %em needs
            to be applied. So in the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi App
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
            example, it
            %em synthesizes
            only
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B[N/x]
            %em checks against
            any type judgmentally equal to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B[N/x]
          %p
            Type theorists have a standard technique for distinguishing these two situations and controlling the use of the conversion rule, known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bidirectional+typechecking"} bidirectional typechecking
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma\vdash T:A
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T \Rightarrow A
              \: in context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              the term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %em synthesizes
              the type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is, if it exists, uniquely determined by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T \Leftarrow A
              \: in context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              the term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %em checks against
              the type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              are all “inputs” and the only “output” is the truth value of whether the typecheck is valid.
          %p Whereas unidirectional typing rules have the conversion rule, allowing the type to be changed at any point to a judgmentally equal one, with bidirectional typing rules, conversion is only used when switching from a synthesizing premise to a checking conclusion, using the “mode-switching” rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash T\Rightarrow A \qquad \Gamma \vdash A\equiv B \,type}{\Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow B}
          %p
            Unlike typical versions of bidirectional typing, in our approach the checking judgment is defined
            %em solely
            by the mode-switching rule, and all other typing rules conclude a synthesizing judgment. This variant doesn’t help with typechecking algorithms, but it still factors out type conversion cleanly: the equality judgment is used when, and only when, the mode switches.
          %p
            So in summary, terms formers are fully annotated, their typing rules
            %em synthesize
            a type in the conclusion, and have premises that
            %em check
            their subterms against appropriate types. So the mode-switching rule gets applied a lot, because it’s the only way, in our system, to derive a checking judgment.
          %p
            From a syntactic perspective, there are many advantages of bidirectional typechecking, but it is unclear at present whether any of them are relevant to categorical semantics (although by using a bidirectional framework we are better placed to take advantage of them if they do become relevant). But more importantly, the natural structure of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Initiality+Project+-+Partial+Interpretation"} semantic interpretation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Lumsdaine"} Peter Lumsdaine
          %p
            Typical versions of bidirectional typing also have bidirectional
            %em equality
            rules, which essentially implement an algorithm for equality-checking. (Such partially-type-directed algorithms have advantages over simply normalizing both terms and comparing normal forms, such as more easily incorporating eta-conversion.) However, since we want our theorem to generalize to type theories in which equality is not algorithmic (e.g. theories with equality reflection) — and more importantly, since in semantic equality there is no “output information” to ever compute — we will not orient our equality judgments.
          %p
            Our sort of bidirectional theory is very closely related to a unidirectional theory that may be more familiar to some readers. Roughly speaking, to go from a unidirectional theory to our sort of bidirectional one: make sure everything is fully annotated, make the conclusions of formation/introduction/elimination rules synthesizing and their premises checking, and replace the conversion rule by the mode-switching rule. Oppositely, to recover a unidirectional theory from our bidirectional one, simply collapse both checking and synthesizing judgments into the “term has a type” judgment
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T:A
          %h2#contexts Contexts
          %p
            A
            %strong raw context
            is a finite list of pairs consisting of a variable and a raw term in which the variables occurring are all pairwise distinct. Each such pair, written
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            = succeed "," do
              %strong typing declaration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            being a type to which the variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            belongs.
          %p
            A
            %strong valid context
            is a raw context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_1:A_1, \dots ,x_n:A_n)
            such that each judgment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi k
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi k
                  %mo ⊢
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1:A_1,\dots, x_k:A_k \vdash A_{k+1} \, type
            is derivable by the rules below. This notion is defined
            %em a posteriori
            after the primitive rules for all the judgments have been given. In particular, the judgments themselves never refer to context validity. The intuitive interpretation of a judgment-in-context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi 𝒥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma\vdash \mathcal{J}
            should therefore be “
            %em if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is a valid context, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}
            holds in that context” (and this is the meaning we will give it semantically).
          %h2#judgements Judgment forms
          %p
            Our type theory has five judgment forms. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is a metavariable ranging over raw contexts, while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S,T,A,B
            are metavariables ranging over raw terms.
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A\,type
              — “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a type in context
              = succeed "\u201D." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T \Leftarrow A
              — “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              can be checked to have type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in context
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T \Rightarrow A
              — “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              synthesizes the type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in context
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A \equiv B \, type
              — “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              are judgmentally equal types in context
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi S
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash S \equiv T : A
              — “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              are judgmentally equal terms of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in context
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %h3#modes_preconditions_etc Modes, preconditions, etc.
          %p
            All the metavariables occurring in all of our judgments should be regarded as “inputs”, with the sole exception that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in the synthesizing judgment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T \Rightarrow A
            is an output. In other words, if we regard “searching for a derivation of some judgment” as an “algorithm”, with the rules for deriving that judgment as the “cases to try” and their premises as the resulting “recursive calls”, then in all but one case the algorithm takes all the arguments of the judgment as inputs and returns only a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T \Rightarrow A
            it takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            as inputs and returns
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as output (if it succeeds, otherwise it returns failure).
          %p Note, though, that because our judgmental equality is not bidirectional and need not be even semidecidable, these “algorithms” are not really algorithms, unless we regard the equality judgment as a sort of “oracle” that they can invoke.
          %p
            Similarly, we regard validity of all the “inputs” of each judgment as something that the “caller” should ensure, while validity of the “output” as something that the function returning it should “promise”. For example, the rules for deriving a judgment such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇐
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T \Leftarrow A
            need only be “correct” under the
            %em assumption
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is a valid context and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A\,type
            %em premises
            of such rules hold.
          %h2#primitive_rules Primitive rules
          %h3#structural_rules Structural rules
          %p The main structural rule is the “variable” or “hypothesis” rule, which says that if a variable is declared in a context, then as a term it synthesizes the type that it is declared with:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{(x:A) \in \Gamma}{\Gamma \vdash x \Rightarrow A}
          %p The other structural rules of weakening, contraction, and substitution will be admissible.
          %h3#modeswitching_rule Mode-switching rule
          %p
            The mode-switching rule says that to check that a term has a given type, we first synthesize a type for that term and then check that this type equals the given one. In our fully annotated system, this is the
            %em only
            way to derive the checking judgment
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T \Leftarrow B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash T \Rightarrow A \qquad \Gamma \vdash A \equiv B \, type}{\Gamma \vdash T \Leftarrow B}
          %p This is also the only incarnation of the conversion rule, which in a “unidirectional” system is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash T : A \qquad \Gamma \vdash A \equiv B \, type}{\Gamma \vdash T : B.}
          %p A bidirectional system controls this rule more carefully, enforcing it to be used (when and) only when the mode switches.
          %h3#equality_rules Equality rules
          %p We have primitive rules asserting that equality of terms and types are both reflexive, transitive, and symmetric, and moreover that equality of terms is invariant under equality of types.
          %p
            For equality of types
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
