                    \\frac{
                    \\Gamma \vdash A \equiv A' type \qquad
                    \\Gamma, x:A \vdash B \equiv B' type \qquad
                    \\Gamma, x:A \vdash M \equiv M' : B
                    }
                    {\Gamma \vdash \lambda(x:A.B).M \equiv \lambda(x:A'.B').M' : \Pi(x:A).B}
                    \\\
                    \\\
                    \\frac{
                    \\Gamma \vdash A \equiv A' type \qquad
                    \\Gamma, x:A \vdash B \equiv B' type \qquad
                    \\Gamma \vdash M \equiv M' : \Pi(x:A)B \qquad
                    \\Gamma \vdash N \equiv N' : A
                    }
                    {\Gamma \vdash App^{x:A.B}(M, N) \equiv App^{x:A'.B'}(M', N') : B[N/x]}
                    \\end{gathered}
          %h2#valid_contexts_and_telescopes Valid Contexts and Telescopes
          %p
            A (raw)
            %strong telescope
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is a (raw) context. A
            %strong valid telescope
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                  %mi ok
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta ok
            is defined by another inductively-defined judgment form:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi ok
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{}{\Gamma \vdash \cdot ok}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi ok
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mi ok
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash \Delta ok \qquad
                  \\Gamma,\Delta \vdash A type \qquad
                  x \notin \Gamma,\Delta}
                  {\Gamma \vdash \Delta,x:A ok}
          %p
            Intuitively, a telescope is an open context. So a
            %strong valid context
            can now be formalized as the closed special case of a valid telescope, with the single-rule judgment form:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi ok
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi ok
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\cdot \vdash \Gamma ok}{\Gamma ok}
          %p A valid telescope can also be understood as a valid multi-step context extension. So we have the theorem:
          %p
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mi ok
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma ok
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi ok
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta ok
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi ok
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma,\Delta ok
          %p You can also concatenate telescopes:
          %p
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                  %mi ok
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta ok
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ′
                    %mi ok
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma,\Delta \vdash \Delta' ok
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ′
                    %mi ok
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta,\Delta' ok
          %p In fact, by the definition of context validity, the multi-step context extension is a special case of telescope concatenation.
          %h2#bidirectional_vs_unidirectional Bidirectional vs Unidirectional
          %p
            As noted above, there is a canonical correspondence between our bidirectional theory (with any choice of type formers) and a unidirectional one obtained by collapsing both typing judgments
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇐
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\Leftarrow A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\Rightarrow A
            into one
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T:A
            (and thereby the mode-switching rule into the conversion rule).
          %p
            In fact, we can prove that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇐
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow A
            is derivable bidirectionally if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T: A
            is derivable unidirectionally.
          %p
            The “only if” direction is easy: just change all the
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇐
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Leftarrow
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            %em admissible
            bidirectional rules under
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T:A)\mapsto(T\Leftarrow A)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow A \qquad \Gamma \vdash A\equiv B \, type}{\Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow B}
          %p
            is admissible in our bidirectional theory. But as noted above, any derivation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇐
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow A
            must end with an invocation of the mode-switching rule. So there must be some
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇐
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow A
            was derived by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi C
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi A
                      %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash T\Rightarrow C \qquad \Gamma \vdash C\equiv A \, type}{\Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow A.}
          %p
            So the premises of that rule instance must be derivable. Thus, we can combine the derivations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi C
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash C\equiv A \, type
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A\equiv B \, type
            with transitivity, then apply the mode-switching rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi C
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash T\Rightarrow C \qquad \Gamma \vdash C\equiv B \, type}{\Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow B}
          %p
            to get a derivation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇐
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash T\Leftarrow B
            as desired.
          %h2#admissible_rules Admissible rules
          %h3#inversion_lemmas Inversion lemmas
          %p Inversion lemmas let you “take apart” a derivation by observing that only certain rule(s) could’ve been used as the last step of the derivation, based on the form of one or more objects being judged.
          %p TODO
          %h3#structural_rules_2 Structural rules
          %p
            Writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi 𝒥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \mathcal{J}
            for one of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#judgements"} judgments
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash \Gamma' \qquad \Gamma, \Gamma' \vdash \mathcal{J}}{\Gamma, x:A, \Gamma' \vdash \mathcal{J}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash B\,type \qquad \Gamma, x:A, y:B, \Gamma' \vdash \mathcal{J}}{\Gamma, y:B, x:A, \Gamma' \vdash \mathcal{J}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, x:A, y:A, \Gamma' \vdash \mathcal{J}}{\Gamma, x:A, \Gamma'[x/y] \vdash \mathcal{J}[x/y]}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi 𝒥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash T \Rightarrow A \qquad \Gamma, x:A, \Gamma' \vdash \mathcal{J}}{\Gamma, \Gamma'[T/x] \vdash \mathcal{J}[T/x]}
          %p (TODO)
          %h3#sanity_check Sanity check
          %p Sanity checks are theorems about the formal judgments that provide some confidence that the rules are propagating validity presuppositions correctly. Since we have only one judgment form that establishes any of its presuppositions, there seems to be only one sanity check we can do:
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mi ok
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma ok
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash M \Rightarrow A
            then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A type
          %p
            Intuitively, a derivation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash M \Rightarrow A
            somehow contains a derivation of the presupposition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi ok
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma ok
          %p
            The proof is by induction on the main derivation—of
            = succeed "\u2014and" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash M \Rightarrow A
          %p TODO
